  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Unusually warm weather is expected Today and \nWednesday, with forecast temperatures potentially reaching record \nhighs for early November.\n\nA mid-level ridge will continue to build east across the Atlantic \nseaboard through mid-week, supporting surface high pressure to \npersist off the eastern Atlantic. Overall this will support \ngenerally dry weather, however weak warm air advection and \nisentropic lift associated with a passing weak warm front may \ngenerate a few widely scattered showers across western New York this \nmorning, reaching and then passing across eastern New York late this \nmorning through this afternoon. In the wake of the front, dry air \nadvection and subsidence will support a decrease in cloud cover from \nwest to east Tuesday afternoon through Tuesday night. A 25 to 30 \nknot low-level jet will move into the western Southern Tier tonight, \nsupporting a south-southeast surface wind within a modest inversion. \nAs such, gusty downslope winds of up to 30mph along and to the lee \nside of the Chautauqua Ridge is likely. Gusts will lessen on the lee \nside of the Ridge Wednesday.\n\nDespite clouds and a few showers impacting the region today, 850mb \ntemperatures are expected to reach +12C, supporting maximum \ntemperatures to reach the low 70s across western New York and the \nmid 60s across north-central New York. As the aforementioned ridge \nbuilds further north Wednesday, 850mb temperatures will \ncontinue to rise, peaking to +13C. As such another very warm day\nfor early November is expected, where maximum temperatures will\nrange in the mid to upper 70s. As winds are forecast to remain \nsoutherly, lake breeze influences will likely be limited and \ntherefor allow temperatures to soar well above climatological \nnorms. See Climate section for more information regarding record\ntemperatures. \n\nCooler air will build into the region from west to east Thursday, \nhowever it will remain warm especially from the Finger Lakes region \ninto north-central NY. Cooler weather, although still above normal \nis expected Friday into the weekend.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...High probability for widespread showers Wednesday \nnight through Thursday due to the passage of a cold front. Unsettled \nweather is expected to continue into the weekend.  \n\nDeep troughing and associated closed low will move into the Great \nLakes for the later half of the work week. A deep southerly flow \noriginating from the Gulf and southern Atlantic Ocean will support a \nplume of moisture into the eastern Great Lakes region Wednesday \nnight through Thursday morning. This deep moisture combined with \nforcing from the surface to 500mb due to a passing cold front will \nsupport widespread showers across the region, and provide some much \nneeded relief to the recent dry spell. However should note that the \nshower's residence timing will be quick, as the plume of \nmoisture will move east of the region by Thursday afternoon. \nForecast rainfall amounts will generally be less than a quarter \nof an inch by Thursday afternoon.\n\nIn the wake of the cold front, expect gusty southwest winds \nnortheast of Lake Erie with gusts of 25 to 30 mph impacting much of \nthe Niagara Frontier. \n\nThe trough will move overhead Friday and this weekend, where another \ncold front is progged to pass across the region, where chances for \nshowers are possible.",
